"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,235\nEpisode 1\n\n3\n00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:09,348\nWords and pictures\n\n4\n00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:15,751\nHere is a short explanation of how a bicycle pump works,\n\n5\n00:00:15,941 --> 00:00:17,035\nin words alone.\n\n6\n00:00:17,477 --> 00:00:18,722\nNow the same words,\n\n7\n00:00:18,742 --> 00:00:20,338\nwith a simple diagram.\n\n8\n00:00:20,658 --> 00:00:23,083\nWhich one will students understand better?\n\n9\n00:00:23,253 --> 00:00:27,941\nRichard Mayer's theory of multimedia learning starts from three ideas.\n\n10\n00:00:28,276 --> 00:00:30,701\nWe take in information through two channels,\n\n11\n00:00:30,931 --> 00:00:33,373\none for words and one for pictures.\n\n12\n00:00:33,816 --> 00:00:36,050\nEach channel has limited capacity.\n\n13\n00:00:36,392 --> 00:00:39,405\nAnd learning happens when we actively connect the two.\n\n14\n00:00:39,575 --> 00:00:41,436\nAcross decades of experiments,\n\n15\n00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:48,169\nstudents who learned from words and pictures did better on transfer tests than students who got words alone.\n\n16\n00:00:48,793 --> 00:00:51,335\nIn Mayer's 2021 summary,\n\n17\n00:00:51,619 --> 00:00:54,919\nthe median effect size was one point three five,\n\n18\n00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:59,175\nand the result was positive in thirteen of thirteen tests.\n\n19\n00:00:59,345 --> 00:01:01,003\nNot just any picture helps.\n\n20\n00:01:01,331 --> 00:01:04,955\nThe useful ones show how something works or how parts relate: a diagram,\n\n21\n00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:07,908\na process, a graph.\n\n22\n00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:10,401\nDecorative images do not count,\n\n23\n00:01:10,701 --> 00:01:12,271\nand as the next episode shows,\n\n24\n00:01:12,291 --> 00:01:13,743\nthey can get in the way.\n\n25\n00:01:13,913 --> 00:01:15,054\nThere is a boundary.\n\n26\n00:01:15,402 --> 00:01:18,728\nThe effect is stronger for novices than for experts.\n\n27\n00:01:19,112 --> 00:01:25,364\nStudents who already know a topic can build the picture in their heads; beginners need it on the page.\n\n28\n00:01:25,534 --> 00:01:27,391\nThis is not learning styles.\n\n29\n00:01:27,785 --> 00:01:30,926\nEvery student benefits from words and pictures together,\n\n30\n00:01:31,181 --> 00:01:34,700\nbecause the content has both a verbal and a visual side,\n\n31\n00:01:35,002 --> 00:01:36,996\nas we saw in Series six.\n\n32\n00:01:37,166 --> 00:01:37,945\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:38,344 --> 00:01:43,142\nPair your key explanations with a simple diagram that shows how the parts connect.\n\n34\n00:01:43,676 --> 00:01:45,502\nPut labels on the diagram,\n\n35\n00:01:45,526 --> 00:01:47,048\nclose to what they describe.\n\n36\n00:01:47,590 --> 00:01:50,576\nGive beginners more visuals than advanced students.\n\n37\n00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:55,473\nOne tip to share with students: sketch a diagram next to your notes.\n\n38\n00:01:55,643 --> 00:01:56,330\nWords tell.\n\n39\n00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:57,212\nPictures show.\n\n40\n00:01:57,232 --> 00:01:58,765\nTogether, they explain.\n\n"